 {"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "description": "Use when solving ARC-style grid transformation puzzles.", "tags": [], "related_skills": [], "content": "---\nname: arc-grid-reasoning\ndescription: Use when solving ARC-style grid transformation puzzles.\n---\n\n# ARC grid reasoning\n\nUse this skill for ARC-style tasks where an input grid is transformed according to one or more demonstrations. The objective is to infer a rule that explains every observed pair, then apply it exactly to the test grid.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Record the evidence.** Note grid dimensions, colors, connected components, repeated motifs, boundaries, and unusual cells. Keep row/column indexing explicit (prefer zero-based coordinates in scratch work, but state it).\n2. **Compare input and output cell-by-cell.** Identify every changed cell and every preserved structure. Separate facts directly demonstrated from hypotheses about why they changed.\n3. **Describe the rule at object level.** Consider components, enclosure, alignment, symmetry, repetition, color roles, and local/global context. Avoid a rule such as “remove odd pixels” unless the examples establish what qualifies as odd and what happens to each such cell.\n4. **Test the rule against all demonstrations.** For each candidate, predict the output of every example before applying it to the test. Reject a candidate that needs unexplained exceptions.\n5. **Construct the test output conservatively.** Start from the input and edit only cells/objects justified by the inferred rule. Preserve all other cells. Check dimensions and row lengths before submission.\n6. **Use demonstrations strategically.** If a submission is incorrect, treat that as evidence against the complete predicted grid, not proof that one visually suspicious cell is the only error. Reassess the rule and compare the prior hypothesis against the demo evidence. When several explanations remain plausible, request another demonstration rather than making near-identical guesses. A demo request has a known cost, so weigh it against the cost and information value of another guess.\n7. **Track hypotheses and attempts.** Keep a concise change list (coordinates, old value, new value, reason) so near-identical outputs are not repeated. After a wrong answer, identify which assumptions can be falsified and what new evidence would distinguish them.\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- A color that appears as a singleton in one demonstration may be a marker or noise only in a particular context; do not generalize from color or apparent isolation alone.\n- Do not assume every locally anomalous cell should be replaced by the surrounding color. The transformation may target only one type of object, apply to selected regions, or change the grid in another way.\n- Avoid changing a single ambiguous cell between successive full-grid submissions without a reason supported by evidence; the rest of the grid may also be wrong.\n- Before submission, verify the entire grid, not just the cells changed from the input.\n\n## Supporting notes\n\n- See `references/demo-driven-inference.md` for a compact example of how to treat ambiguous singleton cleanup and wrong-submission feedback.\n", "path": "reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/SKILL.md", "skill_dir": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-medium-fresh0/frozen/seen-reference/play/users/u075/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning", "org_provenance": null, "linked_files": {"references": ["references/action-protocol.md", "references/demo-driven-inference.md"]}, "usage_hint": "To view linked files, call skill_view(name, file_path) where file_path is e.g. 'references/api.md' or 'assets/config.yaml'", "required_environment_variables": [], "required_commands": [], "missing_required_environment_variables": [], "missing_credential_files": [], "missing_required_commands": [], "setup_needed": false, "setup_skipped": false, "readiness_status": "available", "_source_path": "/home/USER/conti